{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q016_pdb_1acc_domain_split","answer":"Verified SCOPe facts. In SCOP 1.75 (and SCOPe 2.01, 2.02, 2.03) PDB 1acc chain A was one whole-chain domain, d1acca_ (sunid 43828, description '1acc A:'), in class f (membrane and cell surface proteins), fold f.11 (anthrax protective antigen), superfamily f.11.1, family f.11.1.1 (protein and species: anthrax protective antigen, Bacillus anthracis, TaxId 1392). From SCOPe 2.04 onward, and in 2.05, 2.06, 2.07 and 2.08, the chain is split into two domains. d1acca1 (sunid 238521) covers A:226-735 and is f.60.1.1: class f, fold f.60 'Anthrax protective antigen C-terminal-like', superfamily f.60.1, family f.60.1.1. d1acca2 (sunid 238522) covers A:14-225 and is b.179.1.1: class b (all beta), fold b.179 'Anthrax protective antigen N-terminal-like', superfamily b.179.1 'PA14-like', family b.179.1.1 'PA14'. So the single 1.75 domain, which sat in a fold named for the whole antigen, became an N-terminal PA14-like beta domain and a C-terminal domain. The new domains are in different classes, folds and superfamilies. The fold, superfamily and family sunids used in 2.08 (254103, 254122, 254146 and 254104, 254123, 254147) differ from the 1.75 ones (56987, 56988, 56989), so these are new nodes, not a relabeling. I queried 2.04 through 2.07 and the split appears first in 2.04, but I did not retrieve the 2.04 hierarchy or a history record. Release 2.08 homology and annotation responses show no warnings, heterogeneity, repeat annotations or inconsistencies for either domain. Both carry only the comment 'complexed with ca'. The API does not expose the curators' rationale, so why the split was made (structural analysis, literature, or a new PA14 grouping) is not verified.

What it reveals. The domain, not the chain, is the unit of classification. A chain can be a fusion of independently folding, evolutionarily separable modules with different relatives. The 1.75 single-domain call hid this. The two segments are different evolutionary modules (the second one is a PA14 module, a family also found in other proteins). Placing them in different superfamilies means SCOPe makes no positive common-ancestry assertion between them. It does not prove they are unrelated, analogous or convergent. The domain-level classification is a revisable hypothesis that improves as structures and methods improve.

Effect on homology searches. (1) Do not assign one family or superfamily to a whole chain. Look up the domains of the chain in the specific release (/pdb/1acc/domains), and do not assume chain = domain. (2) Use the current domain boundaries and sids, and record the release. Legacy identifiers (d1acca_, f.11.1.1) are obsolete, and mixing 1.75 with 2.04 or later gives wrong lineages and wrong counts. (3) Search and align each domain separately (A:14-225 and A:226-735). A hit to only one segment gives homology evidence only for that segment, not for the full chain, so check which region the hit covers and require a plausible domain-level coverage. Full-length searches can produce partial matches that say nothing about the other module. (4) Domain-level homology does not transfer function, ligand binding, mechanism or template suitability. Superfamily or family placement is not enough to infer a specific substrate or role, and 'complexed with ca' is only a note on the entries. (5) A chain-level BLAST or FATCAT hit is comparison evidence, not a SCOPe assignment. (6) For benchmark or training sets, the split changes redundancy and leakage, because the two domains of one chain can have different relatives and need independent handling in clustering and representative selection.","evidence_used":["/api/v1/releases","/api/v1/releases/1.75/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.01/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.02/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.03/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.04/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.05/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.06/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.07/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/1.75/sids/d1acca_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca2/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca1/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca2/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca1/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sid